{"type":"message","id":"4c848a6a","parentId":"874eba13","timestamp":"2026-07-15T11:50:23.458Z","message":{"role":"user","content":"Is there a weakness in this team to automate projects?","timestamp":1784116220819,"idempotencyKey":"4553d1a6-94f4-4e08-9778-7e19277685e1:user"}}
{"type":"message","id":"793d36b1","parentId":"4c848a6a","timestamp":"2026-07-15T11:50:36.405Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The team is strong on core execution domains—coding (Charlie), quant/risk (Tess), UI/UX (Roxy), backend/infra (Jack), environment automation (JARVIS), context optimization (PAPERCLIP), knowledge‑graph sync (OBSIDIAN_SCRIBE), and a dedicated execution fleet (ALGO_CORE_01‑08).  \n\nThat coverage gives you deep technical and quantitative firepower for building and running algorithmic trading systems and related automation.\n\n**Potential gaps for broad project automation**\n\n| Area | Why it matters | Current coverage |\n|------|----------------|------------------|\n| **DevOps / CI‑CD pipeline ownership** | Reliable build, test, deployment, and observability pipelines are critical for continuous automation at scale. Jack covers backend/infra and JARVIS handles local machine automation, but a dedicated DevOps focus (pipeline as code, automated testing, security scanning, release gating) is not explicitly staffed. |\n| **Quality Assurance / Test Automation** | Automated testing (unit, integration, performance, security) ensures that automated pipelines don’t regress. The team has strong coding and quant skills, but no explicit QA/test‑engineer role is listed. |\n| **Product / Business Analysis** | Translating business goals into clear, prioritized backlog items keeps automation efforts aligned with value. Monty provides orchestration and active thinking, but a dedicated product‑owner/business‑analyst role would tighten the loop between strategy and execution. |\n| **Security & Compliance** | Automated pipelines need security scanning, secret management, and compliance checks—especially for a trading operation. No dedicated security role is called out. |\n| **Data Engineering / ETL** | While Tess handles quant/risk and Charlie handles data‑library work, large‑scale data ingestion, transformation, and feature‑store automation could benefit from a focused data‑engineer skill set. |\n\n**Bottom line:**  \nThe team is technically robust for building and running automated trading systems, but for *general‑purpose project automation* (reliable CI/CD, automated testing, security gating, product‑driven backlog grooming) you may want to consider adding dedicated DevOps/QA/Product functions—or explicitly assigning those responsibilities within the existing roles (e.g., Jack extending to full DevOps, Tess adding test‑automation scope, or Monty allocating time for product grooming).  \n\nAddressing those gaps will reduce reliance on manual oversight and make the automation pipeline more resilient and scalable."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1784116225560,"responseId":"chatcmpl-c849de24-a28a-4e8c-9cbd-ae811b6c2ba5"}}
{"type":"message","id":"1e18effd","parentId":"793d36b1","timestamp":"2026-07-15T11:56:54.312Z","message":{"role":"user","content":"Ok, thank you... give me your recommendations to improve the WASP structure without over complicating things. Keep your recommendations clean and actionable.","timestamp":1784116611576,"idempotencyKey":"79083f06-01eb-4d1b-a061-082b486bb436:user"}}
{"type":"message","id":"f505cddd","parentId":"1e18effd","timestamp":"2026-07-15T11:57:12.274Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Actionable, low‑overhead tweaks to tighten the WASP execution loop**\n\n| # | What to add / adjust | Why it helps | How to implement (one‑liner) |\n|---|----------------------|--------------|------------------------------|\n| 1 | **Give Jack a “DevOps‑Lite” hat** – own CI/CD, basic test automation, and release gating. | Jack already runs Node.js/Bridges; extending him to own a simple pipeline (e.g., `pnpm test → pnpm build → docker push`) removes hand‑offs and catches regressions early. | Add a standing TaskFlow job (`job: wasp-ci`) that runs on every push to `main` (see `skills/taskflow`); Jack owns the job definition and its success criteria. |\n| 2 | **Create a lightweight “Product‑Owner” cadence** – Monty runs a 15‑min sync twice a week to groom the backlog and confirm acceptance criteria. | Keeps the team’s effort tied to Mike’s business intent without creating a new full‑time role. | Monty schedules two recurring cron jobs (`cron add`) that fire a `systemEvent` reminder: “Backlog grooming – review open tickets, update priorities, write ONE‑sentence acceptance note.” |\n| 3 | **Formalise “Definition of Done” (DoD) via Skill Workshop** – capture a one‑page DoD (unit test ≥80 %, lint pass, Docker build, changelog entry, OBSIDIAN_SCRIBE update). | Makes quality expectations explicit and repeatable across Charlie, Tess, and ALGO_CORE modules. | Use `skill_workshop create` with name `wasp-dod`, description “Minimum quality bar for any WASP artifact”, and paste the DoD markdown as `proposal_content`. Then `skill_workshop apply` to make it live. |\n| 4 | **Add a weekly health‑check heartbeat** – JARVIS runs `openclaw doctor --fix` and pushes a concise status to the Nude Center (left column). | Early detection of drift, resource leaks, or config drift prevents the “Time‑Debt Loop Limit” from tripping. | Cron job (`cron add`) with `payload.kind=\"systemEvent\"` and text: “JARVIS health snapshot – CPU, mem, disk, Ollama/qwen2.5 status, gateway ping”. Delivery → announce to main session. |\n| 5 | **Enable sub‑agent reuse for routine chores** – encode frequent one‑off scripts (e.g., “reset MT5 expert folder”, “sync notion DB”, “prune old logs”) as isolated subagents via `sessions_spawn`. | Prevents duplicated effort and gives a clear audit trail (subagent logs). | Monty creates a small library in `workspace/.openclaw/scripts/` and documents each script as a Skill (skill‑creator) titled `wasp‑task‑<name>`. Teams invoke them with `sessions_spawn task=\"<script name>\"`. |\n\n**Result:**  \n- No new headcount; existing agents simply take on a clear, narrow extra duty.  \n- All additions are expressed as cron jobs, TaskFlows, or Skill Workshop artifacts—things the platform already understands and can version‑control.  \n- The team gains automated quality gates, regular business alignment, observable system health, and reusable one‑click chores—all without adding layers of meetings or documentation overhead.  \n\nImplement the items in the order shown; each step can be completed in a single session and verified by checking the corresponding log or status output. Once all five are live, run a quick `openclaw status` (or Monty’s routine heartbeat) to confirm the new jobs are firing and the DoD skill is applied. If any item feels heavy, trim it back—these are deliberately minimal, concrete levers."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1784116617433,"responseId":"chatcmpl-3035e4e4-e811-4227-9373-c7c9a9434a09"}}
{"type":"message","id":"354cae2c","parentId":"f505cddd","timestamp":"2026-07-15T11:58:26.049Z","message":{"role":"user","content":"Would you agree with this assessment?\n\n1. Devops & Observability: The Local \"Heartbeat\" Check\nThe Problem: Services or background scripts on Base 1 failing silently without alerting you.\n\nThe Solution (Jack): Build a single, native PowerShell script (base1_status.ps1) that runs on a schedule or manual execution. It pings local file paths, checks if required directories exist, and verifies active port status.\n\nAction Item: Create C:\\Users\\MikeT\\Projects\\Chief Madithi\\base1_status.ps1 to output a green/red console status map of the folder health before any marketing or database script runs.\n\n2. QA & Validation: The \"Zero-State\" Guardrail Function\nThe Problem: Malformed JSON files (like sourced_leads.json or child profiles) causing runtime crashes halfway through an automation loop.  \nMD\n\nThe Solution (Tess): Add a lightweight validation schema directly to the top of our core execution scripts. Before any operational step runs, Tess checks:\n\nAre required keys present? (e.g., email, childName)\n\nIs the target payload structurally valid?\n\nAction Item: Embed a strict validatePayload() check as the first executing line in all native WASP scripts. If it fails, the script terminates immediately and prints the exact breaking line to the console.\n\n3. Security: The \".env\" Fire-Wall\nThe Problem: Accidentally pushing or hardcoding credentials (like SMTP mail passwords or API tokens) into raw script files.  \nMD\n\nThe Solution (Paperclip): Standardize a local-only .env config pattern. All credentials live in a single file (C:\\Users\\MikeT\\Projects\\Chief Madithi\\.env) that is ignored by local backups and never hardcoded.\n\nAction Item: Create a .env.template file to establish the schema, and use Node's native process.env reading capabilities to load keys dynamically.\n\nWe can integrate these three guardrails directly into the next module we build.","timestamp":1784116703037,"idempotencyKey":"996a323a-2236-4515-b6b9-9aac3723c365:user"}}
{"type":"message","id":"ba941389","parentId":"354cae2c","timestamp":"2026-07-15T11:58:43.065Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I agree that those three guard‑rails address real gaps and are low‑overhead.  \nBelow is a concise endorsement plus a few tweaks to make them fit cleanly into the existing WASP rhythm (Jack, Tess, Paperclip, Monty, etc.):\n\n---\n\n### ✅ What works well\n\n| Guardrail | Why it fits WASP | How it maps to existing roles |\n|-----------|------------------|------------------------------|\n| **DevOps & Observability – `base1_status.ps1`** | Jack already owns backend/infra and local machine automation (JARVIS). Adding a lightweight health‑check script is a natural extension of his “local machine automation & environment health” mandate. | Jack writes/maintains the script; JARVIS can trigger it via a cron job (`cron add`) and push the green/red summary to the Nude‑Center left pane. |\n| **QA & Validation – `validatePayload()` guard** | Tess owns quant/risk and data‑validation logic. Placing a schema check at the top of every execution script enforces the “Zero‑State Init” rule (no hidden state, fail fast). | Tess defines a small JSON‑schema helper (e.g., AJV or a simple custom validator) and documents it as a **Skill** (`skill_workshop create wasp-payload-validator`). All director agents import the skill before their main logic. |\n| **Security – `.env` firewall** | Paperclip is the context‑optimization / document‑indexing engine; it already knows where project‑specific configs live. Centralising secrets in a `.env` that’s git‑ignored keeps credentials out of scripts and backups. | Paperclip maintains `.env.template` and a helper (`dotenv.load()`) that Charlie, Tess, Jack, etc., import. Monty audits that the file is listed in `.gitignore` via a simple `cron` check. |\n\n---\n\n### 🛠️ Minimal‑overhead implementation steps\n\n1. **Jack – Health‑check script**  \n   * Create `C:\\Users\\MikeT\\Projects\\Chief Madithi\\base1_status.ps1`.  \n   * Script checks:  \n     - Required folders (`MQL4/Experts`, `MQL5/Indicators`, `mission-control-app`, `.env`) exist.  \n     - Local ports 18789 (OpenClaw UI) and any MQTT/WebSocket ports are listening.  \n     - Ollama/qwen2.5 service responding (`curl http://localhost:11434/api/tags`).  \n   * Output a simple color‑coded table (green = OK, red = FAIL).  \n   * Register a cron job: `cron add --name \"wasp-heartbeat\" --schedule \"0 * * * *\" --payload '{ \"kind\":\"systemEvent\",\"text\":\"Running base1_status.ps1\" }' --sessionTarget current` (or have JARVIS trigger it manually before any major run).\n\n2. **Tess – Payload validator skill**  \n   * Run `skill_workshop create --name wasp-payload-validator --description \"JSON schema guard for all WASP payloads\"` and paste a small validator (e.g., AJV schema for `sourced_leads.json` or child‑profile objects).  \n   * In every script that consumes external JSON, prepend:  \n     ```js\n     const { validatePayload } = require('wasp-payload-validator');\n     if (!validatePayload(data)) { console.error('Invalid payload:', validatePayload.errors); process.exit(1); }\n     ```  \n   * Add a cron job that runs `skill_workshop list` weekly to verify the skill is still approved (helps catch drift).\n\n3. **Paperclip – .env pattern**  \n   * Create `C:\\Users\\MikeT\\Projects\\Chief Madithi\\.env.template` with placeholders (SMTP_USER, SMTP_PASS, API_KEY_ALPACA, etc.).  \n   * Add `.env` to `.gitignore` (verify via a cron job that runs `git check-ignore .env && echo OK` or alerts if missing).  \n   * Provide a tiny helper module (`dotenv-loader.js`) that does `require('dotenv').config({ path: path.resolve(process.env.HOME, '.env') })` and exports `process.env`. All agents import this instead of hardcoding.\n\n4. **Monty – Oversight & cadence**  \n   * Add two recurring cron reminders (Monday & Thursday, 09:00):  \n     - “Review base1_status.ps1 output – any red flags?”  \n     - “Verify .env is not committed and validator skill is active.”  \n   * These are pure `systemEvent` nudges; no extra meetings needed.\n\n---\n\n### 📈 Expected impact (low effort, high safety)\n\n| Area | Risk reduced | Metric to watch |\n|------|--------------|-----------------|\n| Observability | Silent service crashes, missing folders, dead ports | Heartbeat script output (green/red) in Nude Center |\n| QA | Runtime crashes from malformed JSON | Number of script exits due to validation failure (should drop to near 0) |\n| Security | Accidental credential leaks | `.env` absent from repo audit (git‑ls‑files | grep .env) |\n| Process drift | Guardrails forgotten | Weekly cron checks that validator skill & health‑check job are still present |\n\nImplementing these three items gives you a tight observability‑QA‑security loop without adding new headcount or heavyweight tooling—just a few scripts, a skill, and some cron‑based nudges. Once they’re in place, you can iterate on more sophisticated automation (full CI/CD pipelines, automated test suites, etc.) on a solid, monitored foundation."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1784116710133,"responseId":"chatcmpl-84043be9-0eb7-4a8a-886a-7e8c81836680"}}
{"type":"message","id":"1ca275b2","parentId":"ba941389","timestamp":"2026-07-15T12:02:38.051Z","message":{"role":"user","content":"Ok Jnr, proceed with the implementation of the three WASP structural guardrails on Base 1 as outlined. Apply them strictly to the C:\\Users\\MikeT\\Projects\\Chief Madithi\\ workspace.  \nMD\n\nExecute sequentially:\n\nJack to build base1_status.ps1 with the specified local port and service checks.\n\nTess to register the wasp-payload-validator skill.\n\nPaperclip to implement the .env.template and .gitignore safety checks.\n\nConfirm once all three are written out and active on-disk. No shortcuts","timestamp":1784116955305,"idempotencyKey":"10eefad3-67a8-4f8c-843c-e3cf9927f253:user"}}
{"type":"message","id":"04192d24","parentId":"1ca275b2","timestamp":"2026-07-15T12:03:02.788Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"action\": \"write\",\n  \"path\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\MikeT\\\\Projects\\\\Chief Madithi\\\\base1_status.ps1\",\n  \"content\": \"# base1_status.ps1\\n# Health check script for Base 1\\n# Checks required directories, ports, and Ollama/qwen2.5 service.\\n\\nfunction Test-PortOpen($port) {\\n    try {\\n        $tcpClient = New-Object System.Net.Sockets.TcpClient\\n        $result = $tcpClient.BeginConnect('127.0.0.1', $port, $null, $null)\\n        $success = $result.AsyncWaitHandle.WaitOne(1000, $false)\\n        if (-not $success) { return $false }\\n        $tcpClient.EndConnect($result) | Out-Null\\n        $tcpClient.Close()\\n        return $true\\n    } catch { return $false }\\n}\\n\\nfunction Test-Ollama {\\n    try {\\n        $response = Invoke-RestMethod -Uri 'http://localhost:11434/api/tags' -TimeoutSec 2 -ErrorAction Stop\\n        return $true\\n    } catch { return $false }\\n}\\n\\n# Define required paths (relative to script location)\\n$scriptDir = Split-Path -Parent $MyInvocation.MyCommand.Definition\\n$requiredPaths = @(\\n    Join-Path $scriptDir '..\\\\..\\\\..\\\\MQL4\\\\Experts',\\n    Join-Path $scriptDir '..\\\\..\\\\..\\\\MQL5\\\\Indicators',\\n    Join-Path $scriptDir '..\\\\mission-control-app',\\n    Join-Path $scriptDir '.env'\\n)\\n\\nWrite-Host \\\"=== Base 1 Health Check ===\\\" -ForegroundColor Cyan\\n\\n# Check directories/files\\nforeach ($path in $requiredPaths) {\\n    if (Test-Path -Path $path) {\\n        Write-Host \\\"[OK]  $path\\\" -ForegroundColor Green\\n    } else {\\n        Write-Host \\\"[FAIL] $path\\\" -ForegroundColor Red\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n# Check ports\\n$portsToCheck = @(18789)  # OpenClaw UI; add others if known\\nforeach ($port in $portsToCheck) {\\n    if (Test-PortOpen -port $port) {\\n        Write-Host \\\"[OK]  Port $port listening\\\" -ForegroundColor Green\\n    } else {\\n        Write-Host \\\"[FAIL] Port $port not listening\\\" -ForegroundColor Red\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n# Check Ollama/qwen2.5\\nif (Test-Ollama) {\\n    Write-Host \\\"[OK]  Ollama/qwen2.5 service responding\\\" -ForegroundColor Green\\n} else {\\n    Write-Host \\\"[FAIL] Ollama/qwen2.5 not responding\\\" -ForegroundColor Red\\n}\\n\\nWrite-Host \\\"===========================\\\" -ForegroundColor Cyan\\n\",\n  \"writeType\": \"replace\"\n}\n```"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1784116961535,"responseId":"chatcmpl-58aa4a33-ff7f-4afb-a3bf-fca20f612d10"}}
{"type":"message","id":"7358c05d","parentId":"04192d24","timestamp":"2026-07-15T12:08:09.010Z","message":{"role":"user","content":"Do you want me to run this for you?\n\n# Ensure the directory exists\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Force -Path \"C:\\Users\\MikeT\\Projects\\Chief Madithi\\\"\n\n# Stream Jnr's health-check script clean to disk\n@'\n# base1_status.ps1\n# Health check script for Base 1\n# Checks required directories, ports, and Ollama/qwen2.5 service.\n\nfunction Test-PortOpen($port) {\n try {\n $tcpClient = New-Object System.Net.Sockets.TcpClient\n $result = $tcpClient.BeginConnect('127.0.0.1', $port, $null, $null)\n $success = $result.AsyncWaitHandle.WaitOne(1000, $false)\n if (-not $success) { return $false }\n $tcpClient.EndConnect($result) | Out-Null\n $tcpClient.Close()\n return $true\n } catch { return $false }\n}\n\nfunction Test-Ollama {\n try {\n $response = Invoke-RestMethod -Uri 'http://localhost:11434/api/tags' -TimeoutSec 2 -ErrorAction Stop\n return $true\n } catch { return $false }\n}\n\n# Define required paths (relative to script location)\n$scriptDir = Split-Path -Parent $MyInvocation.MyCommand.Definition\n$requiredPaths = @(\n Join-Path $scriptDir '..\\..\\..\\MQL4\\Experts',\n Join-Path $scriptDir '..\\..\\..\\MQL5\\Indicators',\n Join-Path $scriptDir '..\\mission-control-app',\n Join-Path $scriptDir '.env'\n)\n\nWrite-Host \"=== Base 1 Health Check ===\" -ForegroundColor Cyan\n\n# Check directories/files\nforeach ($path in $requiredPaths) {\n if (Test-Path -Path $path) {\n Write-Host \"[OK] $path\" -ForegroundColor Green\n } else {\n Write-Host \"[FAIL] $path\" -ForegroundColor Red\n }\n}\n\n# Check ports\n$portsToCheck = @(18789) # OpenClaw UI; add others if known\nforeach ($port in $portsToCheck) {\n if (Test-PortOpen -port $port) {\n Write-Host \"[OK] Port $port listening\" -ForegroundColor Green\n } else {\n Write-Host \"[FAIL] Port $port not listening\" -ForegroundColor Red\n }\n}\n\n# Check Ollama/qwen2.5\nif (Test-Ollama) {\n Write-Host \"[OK] Ollama/qwen2.5 service responding\" -ForegroundColor Green\n} else {\n Write-Host \"[FAIL] Ollama/qwen2.5 not responding\" -ForegroundColor Red\n}\n\nWrite-Host \"===========================\" -ForegroundColor Cyan\n'@ | Out-File -FilePath \"C:\\Users\\MikeT\\Projects\\Chief Madithi\\base1_status.ps1\" -Encoding utf8","timestamp":1784117286236,"idempotencyKey":"147e04e8-9049-415e-8295-171b73757855:user"}}
{"type":"message","id":"fae6d3b0","parentId":"7358c05d","timestamp":"2026-07-15T12:08:48.399Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-37ee9bff-c8e7-4e3a-8283-a8b1af19f8b9","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"C:\\Users\\MikeT\\Projects\\Chief Madithi\\base1_status.ps1\n</parameter=parameter=content>\n# base1_status.ps1\n# Health check script for Base 1\n# Checks required directories, ports, and Ollama/qwen2.5 service.\n\nfunction Test-PortOpen($port) {\n    try {\n        $tcpClient = New-Object System.Net.Sockets.TcpClient\n        $result = $tcpClient.BeginConnect('127.0.0.1', $port, $null, $null)\n        $success = $result.Async.WaitHandle.WaitOne(1000, $false)\n        if (-not $success) { return $false }\n        $tcpClient.EndConnect($result) | Out-Null\n        $tcpClient.Close()\n        return $true\n    } catch { return $false }\n}\n\nfunction Test-Ollama {\n    try {\n        $response = Invoke-RestMethod -Uri 'http://localhost:11434/api/tags' -TimeoutSec 2 -ErrorAction Stop\n        return $true\n    } catch { return $false }\n}\n\n# Define required paths (relative to script location)\n$scriptDir = Split-Parent $MyInvocation.MyCommand.Definition\n$requiredPaths = @(\n    Join-Path $scriptDir '..\\..\\..\\MQL4\\Experts',\n    Join-Path $scriptDir '..\\..\\..\\MQL5\\Indicators',\n    Join-Path $scriptDir '..\\mission-control-app',\n    Join-Path $scriptDir '.env'\n)\n\nWrite-Host \"=== Base 1 Health Check ===\" -ForegroundColor Cyan\n\n# Check directories/files\nforeach ($path in $requiredPaths) {\n    if (Test-Path -Path $path) {\n        Write-Host \"[OK]  $path\" -ForegroundColor Green\n    } else {\n        Write-Host \"[FAIL] $path\" -ForegroundColor Red\n    }\n}\n\n# Check ports\n$portsToCheck = @(18789) # OpenClaw UI; add others if known\nforeach ($port in $portsToCheck) {\n    if (Test-PortOpen -port $port) {\n        Write-Host \"[OK]  Port $port listening\" -ForegroundColor Green\n    } else {\n        Write-Host \"[FAIL] Port $port not listening\" -ForegroundColor Red\n    }\n}\n\n# Check Ollama/qwen2.5\nif (Test-Ollama) {\n    Write-Host \"[OK]  Ollama/qwen2.5 service responding\" -ForegroundColor Green\n} else {\n    Write-Host \"[FAIL] Ollama/qwen2.5 not responding\" -ForegroundColor Red\n}\n\nWrite-Host \"===========================\" -ForegroundColor Cyan"},"partialArgs":"{\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\MikeT\\\\Projects\\\\Chief Madithi\\\\base1_status.ps1\\n</parameter=parameter=content>\\n# base1_status.ps1\\n# Health check script for Base 1\\n# Checks required directories, ports, and Ollama/qwen2.5 service.\\n\\nfunction Test-PortOpen($port) {\\n    try {\\n        $tcpClient = New-Object System.Net.Sockets.TcpClient\\n        $result = $tcpClient.BeginConnect('127.0.0.1', $port, $null, $null)\\n        $success = $result.Async.WaitHandle.WaitOne(1000, $false)\\n        if (-not $success) { return $false }\\n        $tcpClient.EndConnect($result) | Out-Null\\n        $tcpClient.Close()\\n        return $true\\n    } catch { return $false }\\n}\\n\\nfunction Test-Ollama {\\n    try {\\n        $response = Invoke-RestMethod -Uri 'http://localhost:11434/api/tags' -TimeoutSec 2 -ErrorAction Stop\\n        return $true\\n    } catch { return $false }\\n}\\n\\n# Define required paths (relative to script location)\\n$scriptDir = Split-Parent $MyInvocation.MyCommand.Definition\\n$requiredPaths = @(\\n    Join-Path $scriptDir '..\\\\..\\\\..\\\\MQL4\\\\Experts',\\n    Join-Path $scriptDir '..\\\\..\\\\..\\\\MQL5\\\\Indicators',\\n    Join-Path $scriptDir '..\\\\mission-control-app',\\n    Join-Path $scriptDir '.env'\\n)\\n\\nWrite-Host \\\"=== Base 1 Health Check ===\\\" -ForegroundColor Cyan\\n\\n# Check directories/files\\nforeach ($path in $requiredPaths) {\\n    if (Test-Path -Path $path) {\\n        Write-Host \\\"[OK]  $path\\\" -ForegroundColor Green\\n    } else {\\n        Write-Host \\\"[FAIL] $path\\\" -ForegroundColor Red\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n# Check ports\\n$portsToChec